  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nDrier and warmer conditions return Tuesday as a shortwave trough \ndeparts to our northeast. Heights increase a touch behind the \nshortwave, resulting in a brief warming trend Tuesday and Wednesday. \nTemperatures Tuesday afternoon are forecast to reach the mid 70s to \nlow 80s, then increase a few degrees further Wednesday afternoon. \nUpslope flow may aid in the development of an isolated shower or \nstorm near the Davis Mountains Tuesday afternoon. Otherwise, most \nlocations remain dry.\n\nWednesday into Thursday, a broad upper trough overspreading the \nwestern CONUS deepens. Through the remainder of the work week, \nsouthwesterly flow increases aloft, accompanied by several shortwave \ndisturbances. Increased moisture and lift from these disturbances \nyield the return of rain chances early Thursday into Friday night. \nThursday night currently looks to feature our best shot at \nwidespread rainfall. Ensembles depict PWAT values peaking between 1-\n1.5\" during this timeframe, over the 90th percentile, suggesting the \npotential for heavy rainfall. Consequently, we'll have to monitor \nhow this setup unfolds going forward. Aside from rain chances, \ncloudier skies will contribute to cooler afternoon temperatures \nThursday and Friday, topping out mainly in the 70s (low 80s in/near \nthe river valleys). Temperatures gradually warm up over over the\nweekend as subtle ridging builds in from the south. Lows each \nnight fall to the 50s to mid 60s.",
